<section id="about">
  <h2>What I Do</h2>
  <p>
    For more than five years I've taken products from idea to scale, used by
    hundreds of thousands of people. These days most of my time goes to the AI
    and automation layer underneath them.
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm part of the core AI team at MetaMask Support, building user-facing and
    internal tools across the full AI stack: RAG-powered search, AI agents,
    automated data pipelines, sentiment analysis, and support automation end to
    end.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside my work at MetaMask, I help businesses work out where AI and
    automation genuinely create value, what's worth automating, and how to put
    it into practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also built a framework for using AI more intentionally, after noticing
    what constant AI use was doing to my own thinking. It gives people a way to
    tell what AI should and shouldn't be used for, and how to protect the skills
    and the parts of their work that matter. I work from it when I help
    businesses adopt AI without losing the things that make their work valuable.
  </p>

  <h2>Why</h2>
  <p>
    I want technology to give people a more human life, not a busier one. These
    tools can be very good for the people using them, but only when they're used
    well, and nobody hands you the instructions for that. I learned most of what
    I know the hard way, in my own work first. Giving that away, to businesses
    and to people figuring it out on their own, is what I want this to be for.
  </p>

  <h2>What I Love</h2>
  <p>
    Outside work you'll find me dancing, hiking, travelling, and spending as
    much time outdoors as I can get away with. The rest of it goes to learning
    things I don't strictly need to know, mostly neuroscience and personal
    growth, baking sweets that are somehow still healthy, and I also enjoy
    experimenting with new hobbies and creative projects: painting, pottery,
    candles, book nooks.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was raised on Harry Potter, which probably explains the nature and the
    magic and the learning. Shrek is my comfort movie, which explains why I
    always laugh at wordplay.
  </p>
  <p>Come say hi. I like hearing what other people are working on.</p>

  <div class="signature">
    <p>— Sara</p>
  </div>
</section>

